EXPAND["screen-enclosure-installation.html"] = '''
          <h2>Materials, doors, and Florida wind context</h2>
          <p>Enclosure installation conversations cover more than pretty elevations. Door swing clearances, emergency egress habits, and how the roof sheds water onto fascia all affect long-term happiness. Chris documents those choices early so partner engineering — when required — reflects how your household actually moves through the yard.</p>
          <p>Coastal Pinellas lots may need different corrosion awareness than inland Pasco pads. Hillsborough projects sometimes face HOA packet cycles that stretch calendars. None of that is a reason to skip planning; it is a reason to start with a scoping call that sorts install versus <a href="rescreens.html">rescreen</a> before anyone draws a full custom cage you do not need.</p>
          <p>Bring survey notes, pool equipment locations, and any future spa or outdoor kitchen plans. Those details change post placement more than brochure photos admit. Screen Team keeps the conversation practical so you approve a path that fits budget and permitting reality in your city.</p>
'''

EXPAND["new-lanai-enclosure.html"] = '''
          <h2>From open patio to usable evenings</h2>
          <p>New lanai enclosures succeed when airflow, shade, and bug control match how you entertain. Chris asks about televisions, ceiling fans, and grill exhaust so the screened volume does not fight your furniture layout. If an older screen room already exists with a tired but square frame, he will steer you to mesh replacement instead of a partner-engineered rebuild.</p>
          <p>Pasco and Pinellas HOAs sometimes regulate visible frame colors and roof styles. Share those rules early. A clean scoping packet speeds partner drawings when permits apply and prevents redesign fees later. Call with slab dimensions and two wide photos from opposite corners of the patio.</p>
'''

EXPAND["custom-screen-enclosure.html"] = '''
          <h2>Field realities that force custom design</h2>
          <p>Custom screen enclosures appear on kidney pools, pie-shaped lots, and homes where a dock path must stay open. Chris sketches door vectors against those constraints before anyone commits to bay spacing. The goal is fewer awkward posts in your sightline and fewer water traps on complex roof planes.</p>
          <p>Sometimes the custom answer is hybrid: keep the main cage, redesign a failed connector, and rescreen what still stands true. That approach can save months versus a full tear-down. When stamped engineering is mandatory, licensed partners enter with a clear brief Screen Team helped you build — not a vague “make it nice” email.</p>
          <p>Waterfront Clearwater and Gulfport properties add salt and wind into the brief. Inland Wesley Chapel lots may care more about preserve insects and two-story massing. Tell Chris which pressures matter on your site so the custom plan is local, not generic.</p>
'''

EXPAND["super-gutter-installation.html"] = '''
          <h2>Sizing for sheet flow, not wishful thinking</h2>
          <p>Super gutter installation fails when someone copies a house-gutter detail onto a cage header. Enclosure roofs deliver sudden sheet flow. Chris sizes troughs and outlets for that pulse, then sets pitch so water does not sit and breed mosquitoes along the run. Downspout discharge is planned away from walkways and equipment pads whenever the yard allows.</p>
          <p>Color, hanger style, and whether the run turns a corner at a spa wing are part of the install conversation. If you are also booking a <a href="full-pool-cage-rescreen.html">full rescreen</a>, say so — shared ladder days keep the project efficient. New Port Richey through Tampa routes regularly combine those exterior scopes.</p>
          <p>After install, watch the first heavy storm. If anything looks wrong, call promptly. Early adjustments are easier than waiting through a season of overflow stains on fresh paint.</p>
'''

EXPAND["super-gutter-repair.html"] = '''
          <h2>Reading sag, seams, and storm behavior</h2>
          <p>Super gutter repair starts with how the run fails in weather. Mid-span sag points to hangers or fastener pull-out. Corner drip often means open miters. Overflow at the outlet can be a crushed downspout elbow rather than a trough problem. Chris separates those causes so you do not pay for a full replacement when a section repair restores performance.</p>
          <p>Conversely, if the trough is a skinny profile under a large cage roof, repair is a stall tactic. He will say that plainly and point you to <a href="super-gutter-installation.html">installation</a> or <a href="pool-cage-gutter-replacement.html">replacement</a>. Honest triage is part of why super gutters are a primary Screen Team service rather than a reluctant add-on.</p>
          <p>Bring a photo taken during rain if you can do so safely from indoors. Moving water tells the story better than a dry trough on a sunny estimate day. Pinellas and Pasco homeowners use that trick constantly — it speeds scheduling.</p>
'''

EXPAND["pool-cage-gutter-replacement.html"] = '''
          <h2>What replacement day looks like</h2>
          <p>Pool cage gutter replacement begins with protecting plants and deck surfaces near the header, then removing the failed line without damaging sound enclosure members. Chris checks whether fasteners pulled through soft aluminum or wood trim that needs attention before new hangers go in. Ignoring substrate problems just recreates sag in a year.</p>
          <p>New runs are hung with enclosure volume in mind — the same philosophy as our <a href="super-gutters.html">super gutters</a> hub. Corners, end caps, and outlet drops are detailed so the first summer storm is a proof test, not a surprise. If lanai gutters on the same elevation are half-failed, replacing them in the same visit prevents a waterfall from jumping onto the new cage line.</p>
          <p>Coastal homes should mention salt exposure; hardware and maintenance expectations shift slightly. Inland Tampa cages often fail from oak debris load more than salt. Either way, replacement is the reset when cleaning and caulk have already been tried.</p>
'''

EXPAND["seamless-gutters.html"] = '''
          <h2>Joining house lines to enclosure drainage</h2>
          <p>Seamless gutters shine on long fascias, but Tampa Bay homes with pool cages need a joined strategy. Water leaving a seamless house run can still overwhelm an undersized cage trough two feet away. Chris looks at the whole elevation: where seamless construction reduces joints, and where <a href="super-gutter-installation.html">super gutter capacity</a> must carry enclosure sheet flow.</p>
          <p>Stucco stains under old sectional joints are a classic tell. Replacing those runs with seamless aluminum cleans the look and removes leak points — provided pitch and outlets are corrected at the same time. Seamless alone will not fix a trough that tilts backward into a corner.</p>
          <p>If you are unsure whether your problem is house fascia or cage header, send both photos. Many Clearwater and Largo properties need a short seamless house section plus a true enclosure gutter upgrade. Screen Team scopes that combination without forcing unrelated work on elevations that already drain well.</p>
          <p>Ask about finish options that sit near cage trim colors. Visual continuity matters on open lanai elevations where gutters read as part of the architecture, not an afterthought stripe.</p>
'''

EXPAND["screen-door-repair.html"] = '''
          <h2>Hinged leaves, sliders, and daily traffic</h2>
          <p>Screen door repair succeeds when the door type is identified correctly. Hinged pool cage doors usually need mesh, spline, and latch work. Lanai sliders add track grit, roller wear, and jump-off problems after storms blow debris into the channel. Chris carries the diagnosis language for both so you are not sold a cage-door fix for a slider problem.</p>
          <p>Pet households should consider stronger mesh on the leaf even when walls stay standard fiberglass. That targeted upgrade absorbs claws where they actually land. If the wall beside the door is slack, sealing the leaf alone leaves a light gap — adjacent panel work may be recommended alongside the door.</p>
          <p>Small door jobs still observe the visit minimum on our <a href="pricing.html">pricing</a> page because travel and setup are real costs. Bundling a door repair with window screens or a gutter look often makes the trip more efficient for both sides. Text both sides of the door and the track if it is a slider.</p>
'''

EXPAND["no-see-um-screen.html"] = '''
          <h2>Coastal evenings and preserve edges</h2>
          <p>No-see-um screen earns its keep where standard weave still lets tiny insects through after dusk. Mangrove edges, Intracoastal lots, and homes backing to preserves see the strongest demand. Chris places fine mesh where people sit and enter first, then expands to full-cage upgrades only when the insect pattern justifies the airflow trade-off.</p>
          <p>Expect a slightly denser look through the fabric. Most homeowners adapt within a day; the comfort gain at sunset is the point. If pets are also shredding doors, combine zones: pet mesh on the leaf, no-see-um on seating walls. That hybrid is common on Dunedin and Tarpon Springs jobs.</p>
          <p>Fine mesh is priced as an upgrade line item on rescreens so you can compare against standard fiberglass before approving. Bring your city and a note about when bites happen — afternoon versus after dark changes the recommendation.</p>
'''

